## 2. Talk track
**1. Title.** "Uplift is an AI student-success assistant for Open University module teams. It finds students who may
withdraw or fail while there is still time, shows what happened to similar students before, and drafts a supportive
plan that an advisor reviews."

**2. Problem statement.** Advisors have thousands of records and little time. The brief asks three questions: have similar
students struggled before, which factors are behind poor outcomes, and who should be supported first. Dashboards answer
"who" but not "why", "what happened before" or "what to do".

**3. Business need.** Contacting students early is what changes outcomes, but capacity is limited: if an advisor can call
10% of a cohort, the list must be right at the top. Advisors also need reasons they can defend and messages that are
supportive, never punitive. At week {{ primary_week }}, {{ t.positive_rate|pct(1) }} of students still enrolled will go
on to withdraw; contacting the top 10% by our ranking reaches students who fail or withdraw
{{ pr.learned_weights.at_capacity[0].precision|pct }} of the time.

**4. Solution overview.** Four things in one console: early warning with reasons, a ranked contact list, similar
historical students (hybrid RAG), and a multi-agent support plan with an outreach draft. Plus a copilot, insights and
evaluation. Everything is "as of" a week in the course: weeks {{ weeks|join(", ") }}.

**5. Architecture.** Bottom: the offline build: dataset, preprocessing, one document per enrolment, embeddings, LanceDB
plus BM25, and model training, once per checkpoint. Middle: the request path: React console, FastAPI with input
guardrails, then the LangGraph workflow: evidence, performance, risk, intervention, critic, synthesis. Claude drafts text;
code decides facts. Every LLM step has a computed fallback. Evaluation and the feedback loop sit alongside.

**6. Data flow.** OULAD's seven tables become snapshot features at each checkpoint, using only data dated before that
day. The key rule: we only score students who are still enrolled. If you also score students who already left, ROC-AUC
looks like {{ pa.all_enrolments_including_already_withdrawn.roc_auc|num }} instead of {{ t.roc_auc|num }}, because
{{ pa.already_withdrawn_share_of_test_withdrawals|pct }} of withdrawals had already happened. That inflated number is
useless to an advisor.

**7. Technology stack.** Claude in three tiers by measured need: Haiku parses search requests, Sonnet writes the agent
text, Opus judges. e5-small-v2 embeddings won a five-model comparison. LanceDB is an embedded vector database with
filters pushed into the search. LangGraph runs the agent graph. XGBoost with TreeSHAP for the models. FastAPI, and React
for the console, served by the same process. DeepEval and RAGAS for evaluation.

**8. Agent workflow.** The performance agent summarises strengths and concerns; the risk agent explains the calibrated
risk with precedents; a router decides intervene or monitor; the intervention agent chooses 2 to 4 actions from an
eligible catalogue and drafts the message; the critic checks IDs, numbers and wording in code; one revision, then a
deterministic fallback. Agents talk through a typed shared state with numbered evidence, which is what makes claims
checkable.

**9. Hybrid RAG.** Three channels: behavioural vectors weighted by what predicts outcomes, BM25 over descriptor tokens, and
e5 dense embeddings of narratives. Fused with weighted reciprocal rank fusion, filtered in LanceDB, earlier presentations
only. The retrieved students' real outcomes predict the query student's with AUC
{{ rb.hybrid.neighbour_vote_auc_poor_outcome|num }} vs {{ rb.random.neighbour_vote_auc_poor_outcome|num }} for random.
Plain-English search reaches precision@10 {{ m.text_search_benchmark.parsers.llm.modes.hybrid.precision_at_10|num }}.

**10. Prediction models.** Withdrawal risk at week {{ primary_week }}: ROC-AUC {{ t.roc_auc|num }} on a later, untouched
presentation, against {{ w.baseline_logistic_regression_test.roc_auc|num }} for logistic regression, and calibrated:
expected calibration error {{ t.ece_10|num }}, so a 20% risk means about 20%. The High tier catches
{{ w.test_tiers.High.share_of_all_withdrawals|pct }} of withdrawals while flagging {{ w.test_tiers.High.share|pct }} of
students. Accuracy rises through the course, from {{ m.checkpoint_summary.checkpoints[0].withdrawal.roc_auc|num }} at week
{{ weeks[0] }} to {{ m.checkpoint_summary.checkpoints[-1].withdrawal.roc_auc|num }} at week {{ weeks[-1] }}.

**11. Demo.** Follow section 3.

**12. Evaluation results.** Five layers. Code checks on every output: {{ ag.deterministic.final_plan_valid_rate|pct }} of
plans pass. LLM-as-judge (Opus, a different model): {{ ag.judge.pass_rate|pct }} pass; in an order-swapped A/B the AI
plan is preferred over the computed plan {{ ag.pairwise_llm_vs_deterministic.llm_win_rate|pct }} of the time. DeepEval
faithfulness {{ ag.deepeval.aggregate.FaithfulnessMetric.pass_rate|pct }} pass. RAGAS: precedent syntheses are
{{ rg.precedent_synthesis.mean.faithfulness|num }} faithful to the retrieved cases (hallucination rate
{{ rg.precedent_synthesis.hallucination_rate|pct(1) }}). {{ n_tests }} automated tests run with no network and no LLM.

**13. Responsible AI.** Sensitive attributes are never model inputs and never sent to the LLM; adding them would change
ROC-AUC by only {{ m.ablations.cost_of_excluding_sensitive_attributes.roc_auc|num }}. Every LLM output is checked for
protected, punitive and clinical wording. The fairness audit tests each gap against chance: the disability gap is real and
reported, with a threshold review for the equity office, not hidden or auto-corrected.

**14. Challenges and learnings.** The biggest lesson: honest evaluation first. An earlier version reported 0.90 AUC by
scoring students who had already left. Second: numbers do not belong in text embeddings, so we band them into
descriptors and keep numeric similarity in its own channel. Third: let the LLM narrate and select, and let code decide
facts. Fourth: a feature set that improved validation was rejected because it did not hold on the test presentation.

**15. Thank you.** "Happy to take questions, or show any part of it live."

## 3. Live demo script (2 minutes)
Before the session: `uvicorn src.main:app --reload`, open `http://127.0.0.1:8000/` and the console at `/today`, choose
**BBB 2014J (held out)** and **week 15**. Close other tabs. If the network or the API key fails, set `LLM_ENABLED=false`
in `.env` and restart: every feature still works with computed text (labelled "Computed").

1. **Landing page** (5 s): "Live numbers from the API; the dome is real weekly activity of students who passed and withdrew."
2. **This week** (20 s): the course clock across the top; click week 5 then back to week 15 and point out that every number
   changes; show the High-tier count, expected withdrawals and "Who to reach first".
3. **Student case file** (40 s): open the first student. Why flagged: drivers. Across the course: risk at each week. Click
   **Draft a support plan**: the agent steps appear one by one; show actions, the outreach message labelled "Drafted by AI",
   rewrite it as an SMS, and the critic's checks. Scroll to **Similar students**: what happened to them next.
4. **Case finder** (25 s): click the example "students who stopped turning in their coursework": the request is understood as
   filters and behaviours; results with outcomes and trajectories.
5. **Copilot** (20 s): ask "Who should I contact first in BBB 2014J?": tool calls appear, the answer streams, IDs link to
   case files, "Every number checked".
6. **Model quality** (10 s): AI features tab: judge, DeepEval and RAGAS results, if time allows.

