## 5. Glossary
| Term | Plain meaning |
|---|---|
| Checkpoint | A point in the course (share of its length) at which we take a snapshot and predict; here weeks {{ weeks|join(", ") }} |
| In scope | Still enrolled at the checkpoint; the only students we score |
| ROC-AUC | Chance that a random student who withdraws is ranked above one who does not (0.5 = random, 1 = perfect) |
| PR-AUC | Precision averaged over recall; compare it with the base rate, not with 1 |
| ECE | Expected calibration error: average gap between predicted and observed rates |
| Lift | Precision at a capacity divided by the base rate |
| TreeSHAP | Exact per-feature contributions to one prediction of a tree model |
| BM25 | Classic keyword ranking function used by search engines |
| RRF | Reciprocal rank fusion: combine ranked lists by summing 1/(k + rank); no score calibration needed |
| Neighbour-vote AUC | Use the retrieved students' real outcomes as a prediction for the query student; its AUC measures retrieval quality without circular labels |
| nDCG@10 | Ranking quality of the top 10 against an ideal order |
| Faithfulness (RAGAS / DeepEval) | Share of claims in the answer that the context supports |
| LLM-as-judge | A separate model scoring outputs against a rubric |
| Order-swapped A/B | Asking the judge to compare A vs B twice, in both orders, to cancel position bias |
| Cohen's kappa | Agreement between two raters beyond chance |

## 6. Panel questions and answers
### Data and leakage
**Q: Why is your AUC lower than numbers reported elsewhere on OULAD?** Many reports score every enrolment, including
students who had already withdrawn, and use random splits. Scored that way, our model would show
{{ pa.all_enrolments_including_already_withdrawn.roc_auc|num }}. We only score students still enrolled at the checkpoint and
test on a later presentation, which is the situation an advisor is in: {{ t.roc_auc|num }} is the honest number.

**Q: How do you prevent leakage?** Features use only rows dated on or before the checkpoint day; students who left before
it are out of scope; `date_unregistration` defines scope only and is never a feature; `final_result` is only a label; peer
percentiles are computed among in-scope peers at that time; the test presentation is never used for any choice; retrieval
only looks at earlier presentations.

**Q: Why these checkpoints?** Advisors need warnings early, but early predictions are weaker. Four checkpoints (weeks
{{ weeks|join(", ") }}) each with their own model let the team choose; the console's course clock switches between them.

**Q: Why a temporal split instead of cross-validation?** Students in the same presentation share a course and a calendar;
a random split leaks that. Training on 2013, tuning on 2014B and testing on 2014J mimics deploying on next year's cohort.

### Models
**Q: Why XGBoost?** Best ranking on these tabular features, exact TreeSHAP explanations, fast on a CPU. It beats a
logistic regression baseline ({{ t.roc_auc|num }} vs {{ w.baseline_logistic_regression_test.roc_auc|num }}), which we keep
as a check.

**Q: Why calibrate?** Advisors read percentages. After calibration the expected calibration error is {{ t.ece_10|num }}, so
"25% risk" means roughly one in four. Uncalibrated scores would only be good for ranking.

**Q: How are tiers chosen?** On the validation presentation: Medium catches {{ w.thresholds.medium_recall_target|pct }} of
withdrawals; High maximises F1 above that. On test the tiers separate cleanly: observed withdrawal rate
{{ w.test_tiers.High.observed_withdrawal_rate|pct(1) }} High, {{ w.test_tiers.Medium.observed_withdrawal_rate|pct(1) }}
Medium, {{ w.test_tiers.Low.observed_withdrawal_rate|pct(1) }} Low.

**Q: Why is outcome macro-F1 only {{ m.outcome_model.test.macro_f1|num }}?** Four classes with Distinction and Fail
being rare and close to Pass at mid-course. It beats both baselines; what we use from it, P(fail or withdraw), has ROC-AUC
{{ m.outcome_model.poor_outcome_from_p_fail_plus_p_withdrawn_test.roc_auc|num }}.

**Q: Could you get higher scores?** We tried richer features and a larger hyperparameter search. Validation improved but
calibration and macro-F1 got worse on the untouched test presentation, so we rejected them rather than tune on the test
set. The current numbers are close to the ceiling for this honest protocol.

**Q: How does prioritisation work?** A weighted blend of withdrawal risk, fail-or-withdraw probability and actionable
gaps (scores, activity, missed work, recent decline), weights chosen on validation by an exhaustive grid with a
one-standard-error rule that prefers a blend when it is statistically as good as the best single signal.

### RAG and retrieval
**Q: What is your chunking strategy?** One document per enrolment, no splitting: a case is a short record and splitting it
would separate signals from the outcome. Numbers are banded into descriptor tokens; a short narrative is embedded.

**Q: Why hybrid and not just embeddings?** Student profiles are numeric; text embeddings do not understand that 45 and 46
are close. The behavioural channel handles numeric similarity, BM25 matches exact descriptors, dense handles free-text
phrasing. RRF combines them without calibrating scores.

**Q: How do you know retrieval works?** We use the retrieved students' real later outcomes to predict the query student's:
AUC {{ rb.hybrid.neighbour_vote_auc_poor_outcome|num }} vs {{ rb.random.neighbour_vote_auc_poor_outcome|num }} random,
on {{ m.retrieval_benchmark.queries }} test students. This is not circular because relevance comes from outcomes, not from
the same features. Typed search has {{ m.text_search_benchmark.queries }} labelled queries.

**Q: Why LanceDB?** Embedded (no server to run), stores the metadata next to the vectors so filters run as a pre-filter
(they never remove true neighbours), exact search at this size, identical results to a brute-force scan in our parity test.

**Q: Why e5-small-v2?** We compared five embedding models on the same narratives: e5-small-v2 had the best precedent quality
and near-best typed search at the same latency as MiniLM; the larger mpnet was slower and not better.

**Q: Why 6 cases in the context and 50 for the outcome mix?** A sensitivity study: more neighbours give steadier outcome
estimates (so 50 for statistics), but a handful of concrete stories keeps prompts readable and cheap (so 6).

**Q: Can retrieval see the future?** No. Only presentations that finished before the query's presentation are searchable,
and the student's own rows are excluded.

### Agents and the LLM
**Q: Why multiple agents?** Each step has a different job and a different check: summarising performance, explaining risk,
choosing eligible actions. Splitting them makes each output small, schema-bound and verifiable, and lets the critic target
the step that failed.

**Q: How do agents communicate?** Through a typed shared state in LangGraph: numbered observations and eligible actions go
in, each agent writes structured output that cites those IDs, the critic writes violations. There is no free-form chat.

**Q: What stops hallucination?** The LLM only sees computed evidence and must cite observation IDs; code rejects unknown IDs,
ineligible actions and any number not present in the evidence; one revision, then the computed plan. RAGAS measures what is
left: {{ rg.precedent_synthesis.hallucination_rate|pct(1) }} for precedent syntheses.

**Q: What if the LLM is down or too expensive?** `LLM_ENABLED=false`: every feature still works with computed text, labelled
as such. Responses are cached, prompt prefixes are cached, a budget cap stops evaluation runs, and the ledger shows cost per
feature.

**Q: Why three Claude models?** Measured, not assumed: for parsing search requests the small model is as accurate as the large
ones at a fraction of the cost; for precedent synthesis the main model had the highest judge pass rate; the judge must be a
different, stronger model so it does not grade itself.

**Q: Why LangGraph and not LangChain or LlamaIndex agents?** We needed explicit control flow (router, critic, revision,
fallback) around our own validated components. LangGraph gives that graph without replacing the parts we measured; a parity
test shows it matches the plain-Python orchestrator exactly.

### Evaluation
**Q: Why both DeepEval and RAGAS?** DeepEval scores the agent analyses and plans (faithfulness, relevancy and custom
criteria such as "supportive and non-punitive"); RAGAS scores the retrieval-augmented outputs against their retrieved
context. Different questions, different tools.

**Q: Can you trust an LLM judge?** We reduce its biases: a different model from the generators, an anchored rubric, the
verdict computed from its scores in code, order-swapped A/B to cancel position bias, and a human-review tab that measures
agreement (Cohen's kappa) once advisors rate cases.

**Q: Why is copilot RAGAS faithfulness lower ({{ rg.copilot.mean.faithfulness|num }})?** Copilot answers are scored whole,
so sentences explaining what a metric means count as unsupported. The numbers in copilot answers are separately checked in
code against the tool results, and the golden questions are {{ e.copilot.llm.answer_correct_rate|pct }} correct.

**Q: What do the tests cover?** {{ n_tests }} tests: every API route, validation and auth, the pipeline's leakage controls,
models, retrieval guards, vector-store parity, the agent workflow with fake LLM outputs (valid, hallucinating, unsafe),
guardrails, the Claude client with a mocked SDK, fairness, feedback and jobs. No network, no LLM, isolated paths.

### Guardrails, fairness and ethics
**Q: Do you use gender, age or disability?** Never as model inputs and never in prompts. They are used only to audit
fairness and as optional filters in case search.

**Q: Is the model fair?** We test each group gap against chance. Region and deprivation gaps are within chance at these
group sizes. The disability gap ({{ fa.disability.equal_opportunity_gap|num(2) }}) is real: disabled students withdraw more
and have more of their withdrawals caught. We show what a per-group cut-off would change, for the equity office to decide;
we do not apply it automatically because that would use a sensitive attribute in decisions.

**Q: Could this be used to penalise students?** It is designed against that: the action catalogue is supportive only,
outreach never mentions risk scores or withdrawal, punitive and clinical terms are blocked, and an advisor reviews every
message. Intended use excludes grading, admissions and sanctions.

**Q: What input validation is there?** API key, admin key for changes, rate limiting, request-size limit, strict schemas
(module and presentation vocabularies, ID and value ranges), injection screening of free text and generic error messages.

### Architecture and engineering
**Q: Why React and not Streamlit?** Streamlit would satisfy the brief; React lets the console do more: per-student URLs,
virtualised tables, streamed copilot answers with tool calls, keyboard and screen-reader support. It is served by FastAPI,
so the demo still runs with one command and needs no Node.js.

**Q: How would this scale?** Models load once per checkpoint; requests are fast except AI drafting, which runs as background
jobs. Several workers on one host share jobs and rate limits through SQLite; beyond that, a job queue and gateway limits
(documented in `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md`).

**Q: How does the feedback loop avoid making things worse?** Proposals need minimum samples, and nothing changes until an
admin applies them; applied changes are versioned and the offline evaluation is re-run.

**Q: What would you do next?** Collect real advisor feedback and human labels, revalidate on newer cohorts, and add an
approximate-nearest-neighbour index if the case base grows.
